# PulseBLR — Environment Variables

# ── MongoDB ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Get a free cluster at https://cloud.mongodb.com
MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://<user>:<password>@cluster0.xxxxx.mongodb.net/pulseblr?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# ── Google OAuth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create at https://console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials
# Authorised redirect URIs: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-client-secret

# ── NextAuth / Auth.js ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=your-random-secret-here
# In production, set to your deployment URL (e.g. https://pulseblr.vercel.app).
#
# NOT OPTIONAL once deployed. Auth.js v5 auto-trusts only Vercel; on any other host
# (Render, Railway, Fly, Docker, a VPS) every /api/auth/* route returns 500 with
# "[auth][error] UntrustedHost: Host must be trusted." auth.ts sets `trustHost: true` to
# fix that, which means Auth.js will believe the Host / X-Forwarded-Host header — pinning
# the canonical origin here removes the inference, so a spoofed header cannot reach a
# callback or an email link. auth.ts logs an error at boot if this is missing in
# production. Reproduced and verified with `next start`.
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── IBM ICA — AI event tagging (FIRST in the cascade, and the only frontier tier) ─────
# The tagger tries ICA → NVIDIA → Anthropic → keyword heuristics. ICA is the only tier
# that can write prose; NVIDIA's 8B cannot, so anything beyond classification needs this.
# These were missing from this file entirely while being the provider actually in use, which
# is exactly how a deploy silently loses its best tier and nobody notices.
ICA_API_KEY=sk-...
ICA_BASE_URL=https://api.nextgen-beta.ica.ibm.com/ica/v1/chat-models
# Verified 2026-08-24: `claude-sonnet-5` answers in ~7s. Send temperature=1 — this model
# rejects 0.2, and ICA reports a MISSING temperature as `{"detail":"Model not found"}`,
# which sends you to check the model name for a fault that is not there.
ICA_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# ── NVIDIA NIM — AI event tagging (SECOND in the cascade) ─────────────────────
# Get your free API key at https://build.nvidia.com → sign in → top-right "Get API Key"
NVIDIA_API_KEY=nvapi-...
# Model to use. PICK THIS FROM EVIDENCE, not from the model list: verified
# 2026-08-09 that `z-ai/glm-5.2` and `meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct` are both listed
# by GET /models on a valid key yet never respond (>25s timeout), while
# llama-3.1-8b-instruct answers in ~376ms. Event classification is a small,
# well-specified task, so the 8B model is the right fit — bigger is not better here.
# Run `npx tsx scripts/check-nvidia-models.ts` to time candidates on your own key.
NVIDIA_MODEL=meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct
# Leave blank to use the default NVIDIA NIM cloud endpoint
NVIDIA_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1

# ── Anthropic Claude — AI event tagging (FALLBACK if NVIDIA key not set) ───────
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# ── Optional: Email digest (Resend) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Sign up free at https://resend.com  →  API Keys  →  Create API Key
RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# 2. Where the daily digest is sent. With the default sender below, this MUST be
#    the same email you signed up to Resend with (test sender only delivers to
#    the account owner until you verify your own domain).
USER_EMAIL=you@example.com
# 3. Sender address. Default (unset) = "PulseBLR <onboarding@resend.dev>", which
#    works with zero setup. To send from your own domain, verify it in Resend
#    (Domains → Add Domain) and set e.g. EMAIL_FROM="PulseBLR <digest@yourdomain.com>"
# EMAIL_FROM=PulseBLR <onboarding@resend.dev>

# ── Admin allowlist (REQUIRED to use the Settings page's controls) ──────────────
# Comma-separated Google account emails permitted to run the scraper, create/edit/
# delete events, and add/remove scrape sources.
#
# Google sign-in is open to anyone with a Google account, so "signed in" is not a
# meaningful bar for operations that affect every user: a stranger could empty the
# event corpus, delete the Source discovery state that took multiple runs to build,
# or loop /api/scrape and burn your LLM quota while getting your IP banned by Meetup.
#
# This FAILS CLOSED. Leave it unset and every admin endpoint returns 503 rather than
# falling back to "any signed-in user" — a deploy that refuses to scrape is much
# cheaper than one where a stranger can delete your data.
#
# The scheduled GitHub Actions jobs do NOT need this: they run `npm run scrape` and
# `npm run send-digest` directly rather than calling the API.
ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com


# ── Web push (OPTIONAL — event reminders that arrive with the app closed) ──────
#
# Mint all four values at once with:  npx tsx scripts/generate-vapid-keys.ts
#
# ROTATING THESE INVALIDATES EVERY EXISTING SUBSCRIPTION. A browser binds its
# subscription to the applicationServerKey it was created with, and the protocol has no
# migration and no dual-key period. Change the key and every stored endpoint keeps
# 200-ing at the push service while the browser silently drops the message (or answers
# 403 VapidPkHashMismatch). Recovery means deleting every row in `pushSubscriptions` AND
# every user visiting Settings to switch notifications back on. Generate once; treat
# rotation as incident response, not hygiene.
#
# Unset is a supported state and fails SOFT, not closed: the Settings section renders
# disabled with an explanation, and `send-push-reminders.ts` exits 0 saying which
# variable is missing. Nothing else in the app changes.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# A SIGNING SECRET. Never reaches the browser, never goes in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable,
# never gets committed.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# REQUIRED BY RFC 8292 AND NOT DECORATIVE: Mozilla's push service rejects a VAPID JWT
# with no `sub` claim, so leaving this unset means Firefox users silently receive nothing
# while Chrome works — a partial failure that reads as a Firefox bug. A mailto: or https:
# URL identifying whoever operates this deployment.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com
# THE SAME VALUE AS VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY, duplicated because only a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable is
# readable in the browser, and the subscribing page needs it as its applicationServerKey.
# A public key is public by construction, so this is not a leak.
#
# IT IS INLINED AT BUILD TIME. Setting it after a deploy changes nothing until the next
# BUILD — not a restart, a build. A build with it unset inlines `undefined`, which the
# Settings section handles by rendering disabled rather than throwing; that is why CI can
# build this repo without ever having a key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
